export function EcommerceSeoAuditEn() {
  return <>
    <p><strong>{"The direct answer: a complete e-commerce SEO audit evaluates everything that influences organic demand and revenue, then ranks actions by business impact. Begin by agreeing on commercial goals: grow revenue in core categories, reduce paid acquisition dependency, or improve mobile conversion on PDPs. Define scope across technical foundations, crawl and indexation, category and product templates, content and intent, international setup, and off-page trust. For Egypt and GCC stores, factor in currencies, taxes, cash-on-delivery nuances, and local shipping expectations because they shape search intent and conversion."}</strong></p>
    <p>{"Before diving into tools, assemble the story. Map the live site architecture versus the intended taxonomy, list winning and underperforming categories, capture recurring customer-service frictions that show up in queries, and benchmark direct competitors on the same intents. Prioritize with a simple rule: fix crawl-blockers and indexation issues first, line up quick wins that move CTR or coverage second, then schedule structural projects. Deliverables should include a prioritized backlog split into quick wins and foundational fixes, content recommendations tied to funnel stages, and a measurement frame that links each task to a clear commercial KPI."}</p>
    <h2>{"Technical foundations: crawlability, indexation, and architecture"}</h2>
    <p>{"Start hands-on: verify bots can fetch and understand resources. Review robots.txt to avoid blocking critical CSS/JS or essential filter paths. Audit XML sitemaps so they only list indexable pages, segmented by type and by language/market where relevant. Use Search Console coverage to surface soft 404s, noindex conflicts, canonical mismatches, and cross-variant loops. For heavy faceted navigation, define rules: sort and filter parameters that duplicate content should be noindexed and, where necessary, blocked, while preserving clean internal links to canonical listings."}</p>
    <p>{"Keep the architecture reasonably flat to reduce crawl depth without losing logical product groupings. Ensure pagination exposes all items to crawlers even with infinite scroll by adding HTML discoverable next/prev paths or a view-all option. Performance matters, especially on mobile category pages. Tame CLS from product images and card heights, defer or remove unused JS, and address Largest Contentful Paint at template level. If you can access logs, inspect how Googlebot spends crawl budget on filters and on-site search results to spot waste early and tighten internal linking."}</p>
    <h2>{"Category and product pages: remove duplication, amplify value"}</h2>
    <p>{"Categories win or lose commercial intent. Maintain a single indexable version without trivial filter parameters that only reorder items. Include a concise, helpful intro that answers buyer questions in Egypt or GCC without pushing products below the fold. When color/size variants get separate URLs, consolidate with a canonical to the primary PDP or refactor the template to host variants on one page. For temporarily unavailable items, keep a 200 status with clear availability and close substitutes instead of deindexing, reserving 404/410 for permanent retirement."}</p>
    <p>{"PDPs need tight metadata and focused headings, plus Product/Offer/Review structured data with correct currencies like EGP or SAR and proper SKU identifiers. Avoid manufacturer boilerplate; write copy that addresses objections and highlights comparable specs. Add on-page Q&A that covers delivery, returns, and warranty. Strengthen internal links from relevant categories and from helpful content to high-margin PDPs. Measure beyond rankings: track impression-to-add-to-cart conversion and average order value from organic landing on each category to decide where development time produces the most revenue."}</p>
    <h2>{"International and Arabic considerations: hreflang, currencies, and trust"}</h2>
    <p>{"Many MENA stores run bilingual and multi-market setups. Start with a precise hreflang map: ar-EG and en-EG for Egypt, ar-SA and en-SA for KSA, and so on, with reciprocal return tags and local canonicals per version. Ensure currency or country switchers don’t silently swap canonical URLs. Country-specific tax, shipping, and returns pages must exist and be current; they influence trust and query behavior."}</p>
    <p>{"Right-to-left layouts can create UX issues that indirectly affect SEO signals. Test filters, nav, and price displays in Arabic. For slugs, choose Arabic or English and stick with a consistent policy; the bigger risk is duplicate variants, not the alphabet. Validate price and availability schema for each market, and verify with Search Console that locale variants aren’t conflicting. Finally, mine on-site search in Arabic to capture Egyptian and Gulf synonyms, then reflect them in headings and filter labels without tipping into keyword stuffing."}</p>
    <h2>{"Content and search intent: from categories to guides and proof"}</h2>
    <p>{"Don’t start with “more blog posts”; start with “cover buying intents.” Build an intent map spanning categories, comparisons, and pre-purchase questions. Practical guides like “how to size an AC for a 20 m² room” support categories while addressing local needs. Link this to an ecommerce growth plan that balances demand creation and bottom-of-funnel capture. On the technical side, make sure ecommerce technical SEO basics are in place so quality content can actually surface."}</p>
    <p>{"User reviews, real-life photos, and clear policies build trust and reduce friction. Deploy FAQPage schema where it truly helps, and avoid pasting the same answers across many URLs. Pass internal link equity from supporting articles to priority categories and PDPs using anchor text that mirrors intent. Monitor thin or overlapping pages within a topic cluster and decide whether to merge or elevate quality based on real demand, not generic assumptions."}</p>
    <h2>{"Measurement, prioritization, and a 90‑day delivery plan"}</h2>
    <p>{"Without rigorous measurement, an audit is a wish list. Attach each recommendation to a business metric: index coverage for a category cluster, CTR gains from improved titles, or LCP improvements on list templates. Use Search Console to group queries by intent, and analytics to track add-to-cart rate and order value from organic traffic. Test major changes at template or cohort level using before/after windows or server-side splits, and be cautious with experiments that alter link structures or core content in ways that may confuse indexing."}</p>
    <p>{"Organize priorities into three lanes: critical fixes to stop leakage, quick wins with visible impact in weeks, and structural projects needing engineering time. A typical 90-day plan starts by removing crawl and index blockers, then upgrades metadata and schema for winning categories, followed by mobile performance fixes. Maintain a simple dashboard showing progress and financial impact per lane, assign a clear owner for every item, and run weekly QA to prevent technical regressions from shipping without acceptance criteria."}</p>
    <h2>{"Links, reputation, and regional signals that matter"}</h2>
    <p>{"Links for e-commerce are not a scoreboard; they’re quality signals when earned legitimately. Leverage vendor partnerships to list your store as an authorized seller with links to relevant categories. Launch genuinely newsworthy assets, like seasonal price trend reports or original stock insights, to earn Arabic media coverage. Local directories, price comparison sites, and regional marketplaces can strengthen brand signals and surface products to new audiences. Focus link equity on strategic landing pages rather than scattering effort thinly."}</p>
    <p>{"Risk management is essential. Avoid private blog networks and shortcuts that invite manual actions. Track branded search growth, anchor distribution, and referral traffic with healthy engagement. Build thorough local shipping and policy pages, and keep customer ratings and support answers visible; these are trust signals even without links. For seasonal content campaigns, align with your ecommerce growth plan and verify ecommerce technical SEO readiness before launch so the payoff arrives as soon as coverage lands."}</p>
    <p>For a related practical guide, see: <a href={"/blog/ecommerce-technical-seo-en"}>{"Related guide: ecommerce technical seo en"}</a>; then <a href={"/blog/ecommerce-growth-plan-en"}>{"Related guide: ecommerce growth plan en"}</a></p>
  </>;
}

