export function InternalLinkAuditEn() {
  return <>
    <p><strong>{"An internal link audit on a large site is about steering both users and crawlers toward the pages that matter while reducing crawl waste. Start by listing the templates that drive revenue or leads, check their current click depth, and surface them with clear, contextual in-body links. For big catalogs, that often means elevating category and brand hubs instead of burying them under thousands of products. For B2B and service networks across MENA, it means strengthening service overviews and city pages, and ensuring every article nudges visitors to a relevant next step instead of ending the journey."}</strong></p>
    <p>{"You will make pragmatic trade-offs: trim a bloated mega menu, add editorial links inside content, and retire weak tag or archive pages. Move weight from repetitive footer links to meaningful in-body references that carry intent. The goal is not more links, but better links that help readers choose and funnel internal PageRank to priority destinations. Expect a cleaner crawl path, faster discovery of new pages, and more consistent visibility for the templates that drive your commercial outcomes."}</p>
    <h2>{"Scope first: crawl, inventory, and normalize"}</h2>
    <p>{"Before changing anything, get a trustworthy inventory. Run a full crawl across languages, subdomains, and only the parameters that can index. Control crawl speed for server health, and enable rendering if navigation is JavaScript-driven. Export page lists and types from your CMS, and use Search Console to confirm top-performing pages and current internal link patterns. Normalize everything: consolidate trailing slashes, ignore hashes, and standardize meaningful parameters. The aim is a clean snapshot you can prioritize against without second-guessing the data."}</p>
    <p>{"Multilingual sites in MENA require extra care. Validate hreflang pairs, and watch for Arabic slugs that accidentally create near-duplicates due to diacritics or inconsistent spelling. Maintain separate sitemaps per language and for key sections. Test pagination and filter combinations with samples, deciding which facets should be crawlable and which must be closed off. Egyptian ecommerce stores often struggle with color, size, and brand filters; keep what has clear search intent and suppress the rest with noindex or robots rules."}</p>
    <h2>{"Find where money leaks and where authority can flow"}</h2>
    <p>{"Click depth is a chronic issue. Pages beyond three or four clicks often underperform. Solve it with visible breadcrumbs, stronger hubs that connect subcategories and explanatory content, and carefully placed in-body links. Hunt for orphaned pages no one links to, then fold them into a relevant cluster or deprecate them. Be wary of infinite scroll that hides important links; lean on clean pagination with text links that crawlers can follow reliably."}</p>
    <p>{"Redirect chains, broken links, and tracking-heavy URLs weaken internal signals. Update links to point directly to their canonical targets, and standardize protocol and host variants. Audit global footer blocks that spray hundreds of links sitewide; most add noise rather than value. Replace them with concise lists and contextual links inside body copy where anchor text reflects user intent and the target’s topic."}</p>
    <p>{"Faceted navigation can silently drain crawl budget. Create a whitelist of facets with strong search demand and promote them to static hub pages with copy, FAQs, and curated links. Block or de-emphasize the rest. For a household-electronics retailer in Egypt, turn key brands into hubs and link to them from buying guides. In B2B, build hubs for core services that point to city pages and case studies rather than spreading authority across weak date archives."}</p>
    <h2>{"Anchor text and mapping: link with intent, not volume"}</h2>
    <p>{"Make anchor decisions from a keyword-to-page map, not guesswork. Assign a primary query and intent to each target page and group related variants. Use a mix of exact, partial, and descriptive anchors, and limit repetition. Account for Arabic and English synonyms and local phrasing. If you want fundamentals, see our internal linking SEO guide. When planning anchors and clusters, our SEO keyword mapping resource helps distribute anchors across topics without stuffing the same phrase everywhere."}</p>
    <p>{"Set lightweight editorial rules. Link from the first mention of the main concept, avoid generic anchors like click here, and never point to deep pagination or transient parameter URLs. From articles, link up to core services or top categories; from categories, link down to best-selling or exemplary items. In bilingual sites, keep anchor semantics consistent in each language, and treat language switchers as complementary to in-language contextual links, not a replacement."}</p>
    <h2>{"Design hubs and information architecture that scale"}</h2>
    <p>{"Define hub templates you can maintain: category, service, city, and brand pages. Each hub should include a short intro that frames choices, curated links to subpages, and stable breadcrumbs. Reduce nav clutter and expose top sellers or popular services inside the main content so those links carry real weight. In retail, brand pages that people search for deserve a dedicated block of editorial links from buying guides. In services, comparison content and FAQs should point to contact forms or chat rather than drifting to unrelated posts."}</p>
    <p>{"Build mobile-first. Links hidden behind deep accordions or delayed renders can be missed or devalued. Keep primary links visible on initial load and use compact link cards with clear headings rather than dense paragraphs without paths forward. Watch for layout shifts that push links around during load, and ensure anchor elements remain tappable. The practical aim is two to three clicks to reach a meaningful decision page in the most common user journeys."}</p>
    <h2>{"Execution and governance across large teams"}</h2>
    <p>{"Split execution into template changes and editorial sweeps. Start with hub templates, breadcrumbs, and curated link modules, then QA on staging against a checklist for targets, anchors, canonicals, and hreflang tags. In ecommerce, implement rules that auto-link brand and category pairs. In B2B, ensure every case study links to its corresponding service and industry pages, and add fixed next-step blocks at the top and bottom of key articles."}</p>
    <p>{"For multi-market teams, publish a playbook: approved anchor types, minimum and maximum in-body links, criteria for selecting targets, and a rollback plan if metrics dip. Assign owners per template and content type, and run a change calendar to avoid peak-season conflicts. Log every internal-link rule with a date and scope so you can trace effects quickly and revert if needed. Training editors on why links are placed prevents later drift back to generic anchors."}</p>
    <h2>{"Measure business impact and iterate"}</h2>
    <p>{"Choose KPIs that represent value, not vanity. Track discovery time for new URLs, growth in indexed key pages, internal click events, and movement for target queries. Use Search Console’s internal links report to spot pages that lost links after a deployment. Where possible, inspect server logs to compare crawl depth before and after. Tie changes to leads, sales, or assisted conversions on priority templates rather than focusing solely on average position."}</p>
    <p>{"Adopt a test-and-expand rhythm. Pilot changes in one category or service cluster, measure a comparable before-and-after window, and scale if positive. If signals turn negative, pause and adjust anchor rules or link placement. Maintain a dashboard that maps each internal-linking rule to its page cohort and revisit monthly to reshuffle priorities by seasonality and stock or service availability. Iteration keeps link equity aligned with what you actually sell or want users to do."}</p>
    <p>For a related practical guide, see: <a href={"/blog/internal-linking-seo-en"}>{"Related guide: internal linking seo en"}</a>; then <a href={"/blog/seo-keyword-mapping-en"}>{"Related guide: seo keyword mapping en"}</a></p>
  </>;
}

