export function LearnSeoRoadmapEn() {
  return <>
    <p><strong>{"The direct answer: anchor your SEO in a clear business goal, then work through a fixed sequence—technical crawlability, keyword research, information architecture, content and on-page, trust signals and link earning, and finally measurement. If you run a Cairo clinic or an advisory firm, start with the two or three services that drive margin and build search-optimized landing pages for purchase-intent queries. Running a small ecommerce store? Prioritize clean category templates and filters before investing in blog content. The plan works when each action ties to a measurable outcome like calls, bookings, or orders—not just rankings."}</strong></p>
    <p>{"In the MENA context, small choices have big impact. Should you be Arabic-only or bilingual? If you sell B2B into the Gulf, bilingual usually wins. If time is tight, tackle money pages first: your highest-margin services or best-stocked categories. Define a six-week sprint with tangible outputs: five fully optimized service pages, baseline Core Web Vitals improvements, and a clear internal linking map. The aim is to avoid scatter, prove business value early, then scale what works."}</p>
    <h2>{"Technical foundations: crawling, indexing, and performance"}</h2>
    <p>{"Check the basics first: can bots reach your key pages? Review robots.txt and your XML sitemap, ensure important pages aren’t accidentally noindexed, and set correct canonicals. Assess link structure, speed, and Core Web Vitals on core templates—home, service, category. Hosting in the region can be uneven; enable a CDN if possible. Start with the easy wins: compressed images, caching, lazy loading, and trimming unused scripts. Enforce HTTPS and keep URLs short and human-readable, avoiding excessive parameters."}</p>
    <p>{"What breaks progress is often subtle: duplicate Arabic/English pages with no hreflang, or faceted filters bloating the crawl budget. Use Search Console to inspect Coverage, Indexing, and Page Experience to surface issues fast. On WordPress or Shopify, rely on reputable plugins but keep the stack lean to avoid JS bloat. Test on mobile first; most traffic in Egypt is mobile. If performance won’t budge, migrate hosting before pouring effort into content that won’t get seen."}</p>
    <h2>{"Smart keyword research for Arabic and bilingual markets"}</h2>
    <p>{"Lead with intent, not volume. Classify queries as informational, commercial, or local. In Egypt, phrasing varies between colloquial Arabic, Modern Standard Arabic, and English transliterations. Build a seed list from service terms and local vernacular, then expand with suggestion tools and customers’ repeated questions. Focus on long-tail queries with clear intent—prices, timeframes, locations. Seasonality matters: Ramadan, back-to-school, and regional holidays shift demand, so plan a content calendar around your commercial peaks."}</p>
    <p>{"Cluster keywords by topic and intent, and map clusters to distinct pages to avoid cannibalization. A primary service page should target the core term; supporting assets can tackle objections, comparisons, and FAQs. If you serve both Egypt and GCC, account for wording differences and localization nuances. Write down your language policy early and decide when English terms are acceptable. Finally, align research with unit economics: if a term requires heavy content, technical fixes, and links, estimate potential revenue before you commit."}</p>
    <h2>{"Information architecture and landing pages built to convert"}</h2>
    <p>{"Structure sets your ceiling. For service sites, build a clean hierarchy: main practice areas, sub-services, then location pages if you cover multiple governorates. For ecommerce, prioritize clear categories without excessive depth. Decide URL policy early: Arabic slugs are intuitive locally but watch encoding and length; English slugs are stable and portable. Implement breadcrumbs, sensible index pages, and avoid unconstrained faceted navigation that spawns thousands of weak URLs."}</p>
    <p>{"Money pages must persuade. Include value proposition, price ranges, timelines, process steps, real photos, and trust elements. Use internal linking from educational content into money pages with relevant CTAs. Monitor bounce rate, dwell time, and organic CTR to iterate titles and meta descriptions. If you operate bilingually, configure hreflang and a clean language switcher to prevent duplicates. Test forms for bookings or checkout across three breakpoints and minimize fields without losing essential qualification data."}</p>
    <h2>{"On-page content that converts and practical E-E-A-T"}</h2>
    <p>{"Start with services and products: answer pricing, timing, process, and expected outcomes directly. Use structured headings, enrich with FAQ and Product/Service schema where relevant, and add original imagery and clear policies. For articles, prioritize topics close to buying intent or early-stage objections. Each piece should have a job: pre-qualify, overcome a doubt, or funnel to a landing page—never publish for volume alone."}</p>
    <p>{"E-E-A-T is pragmatic. Credit a subject-matter author with a concise bio, cite reputable local and regional sources, and schedule periodic updates to keep facts current. Showcase work samples or use cases without inventing numbers. Publish a transparent About and team page, and, for consulting, offer a short discovery form instead of big promises. Track which keywords and pages drive real sales through analytics and CRM, and double down on winners rather than chasing every trend."}</p>
    <h2>{"Off-page and safe link earning without drama"}</h2>
    <p>{"Strong links come from real value. In Egypt and the region, begin with credible local citations, chambers of commerce, and community initiatives that list partners. Collaborate with niche podcasts or industry platforms, and create data-backed or how-to resources that attract citations. Avoid cheap link packs, private blog networks, and sitewide paid links with repetitive anchors. Build a digital PR rhythm: a press note for a meaningful service launch, or an annual market insight report others will reference."}</p>
    <p>{"Measure beyond link counts. Track referral visits, inquiries from specific placements, and unlinked brand mentions. Use UTM parameters for campaigns, and maintain a log of anchors and linking page types. If you inherit spammy links, don’t rush to disavow unless you see clear warnings or harm. A safe alternative to links is publishing genuinely useful reference assets—templates, simple tools, or curated market stats with clear sourcing—that earn mentions organically over time."}</p>
    <h2>{"Measurement, light automation, and your learning path"}</h2>
    <p>{"Agree on practical KPIs before reporting: qualified inquiries, acquisition cost, and order value. Then track SEO indicators: impressions, CTR, rankings by cluster (not vanity single terms), and the share of organic traffic to money pages. Build a simple dashboard and, when feasible, connect Search Console, analytics, and CRM to close the loop. Run lightweight A/B tests on titles and metas for key pages, and keep a change log so you can explain improvements or drops with evidence."}</p>
    <p>{"Continuous learning compounds. Structure your learning in tiers: technical and content basics first, then deepen into schema, log analysis, and advanced research. Work on a small real project—even a side site—to practice and learn from mistakes. When you need structured practice, feedback, and a clear syllabus, join an SEO course that includes projects and peer review. Over time, decide whether to lean technical, content, or analytics. Above all, ship something every week and measure it."}</p>
    <p>For a related practical guide, see: <a href={"/course"}>{"Related guide: course"}</a>; then <a href={"/ar/course"}>{"Related guide: course"}</a></p>
  </>;
}

