export function WhatIsSeoEn() {
  return <>
    <p><strong>{"SEO is the practice of earning qualified traffic from search engines by aligning your pages with how algorithms evaluate relevance, experience, and authority. In practice, engines crawl your site, index content, and rank the best result for each intent. They weigh topical coverage, clear page structure, internal links, external citations, and performance. For a Cairo dental clinic, that means focused service pages for braces, implants, and pricing, marked up correctly and supported by helpful guides. When those pages match what people actually type, and your site is fast and trustworthy, rankings and leads follow—gradually, not magically."}</strong></p>
    <p>{"For service businesses and B2B teams in MENA, SEO is not a replacement for ads, but a compounding acquisition channel with a lower cost over time. Ads provide instant demand; SEO builds durable demand capture on high-intent and research queries. It requires a plan, consistent publishing, technical foundations, and links from relevant sources. The practical decision is to prioritize money pages first—core services, locations, pricing—then expand coverage. The target is not to rank for anything, but to attract visitors who become calls, demos, and signed agreements."}</p>
    <h2>{"Keyword research and intent mapping you can act on"}</h2>
    <p>{"Before writing, build an intent map. Collect terms from your market in Arabic, English, and common transliterations. Segment by intent: informational, commercial, and local. A physiotherapy center in Giza needs distinct service pages for common injuries, educational explainers, and a pricing page. Check seasonal patterns in Egypt and Saudi Arabia, and qualify the first SERP: what pages win and why. Tie each keyword to a single primary page to avoid cannibalization, and note supporting subtopics to cover comprehensively within clusters."}</p>
    <p>{"A common pitfall is chasing broad terms without topical authority or a clear value proposition. A practical fix is to start with long-tail, high-intent opportunities that can convert quickly, then widen the net. Pull questions from sales calls and support tickets; they reveal real search language. If in doubt, test headline angles with ads. Measure success against intent-based KPIs: branded queries, qualified form submissions, and calls from service pages—not raw sessions. When a query yields low-quality leads, reassess its intent match or reposition the page’s angle."}</p>
    <h2>{"On-page SEO that clarifies relevance for people and crawlers"}</h2>
    <p>{"Treat each page as a precise answer to a specific intent. Put the primary keyword naturally in the title, echo it in the H1, and use H2/H3s to structure subtopics. In the opening paragraph, deliver a direct definition or short steps to target featured snippets, then expand. Write meta descriptions to earn clicks without overpromising. Use internal links from strong pages to support newer ones, and interlink topical siblings. Avoid publishing multiple pages for the same intent; when it happens, consolidate instead of competing against yourself."}</p>
    <p>{"Lean into entity clarity: define the brand, services, geographies, and differentiators explicitly. Add appropriate schema for services, products, or articles, but remember structured data amplifies quality—it doesn’t replace it. Keep titles within roughly 55–60 characters to avoid truncation, and ensure the first 150–200 words deliver the substance. Monitor CTR in Search Console for key pages; if impressions are high but clicks lag, test sharper, still-accurate titles. Finally, place compelling, relevant CTAs where intent peaks, not only at the very bottom."}</p>
    <h2>{"Technical foundations: make crawling, indexing, and speed effortless"}</h2>
    <p>{"Before scaling content, run a disciplined technical SEO audit. Start with internal crawl depth and ensure priority pages are within 1–3 clicks. Exclude thin or duplicate pages from indexing, and normalize URL parameters that create copies. Maintain a clean XML sitemap and a purposeful robots.txt. Mobile rendering must be flawless. Speed matters: check Core Web Vitals, compress images, use lazy loading, and serve content via a CDN close to MENA users; regional hosting often reduces server response time meaningfully."}</p>
    <p>{"Watch for JavaScript rendering pitfalls, client-side content that appears post-load, and incorrect canonicals—all can suppress visibility. Fix crawl errors, manage 404s and 301s cleanly, and remove broken internal links. Validate structured data and use it judiciously. Prioritize by business impact: a slow core service page beats cosmetic tweaks on a low-traffic blog. Technical excellence rarely wins rankings alone, but it prevents losing easy opportunities. If you’re unsure where to begin, start with a technical SEO audit and tackle the highest-impact items first."}</p>
    <h2>{"Content and links that build trust and authority"}</h2>
    <p>{"For service businesses, the best pages move prospects toward a decision. Create in-depth service pages with who-it’s-for, process, pricing ranges or packages, and realistic FAQs. Support them with concise case studies showing the problem, solution, and outcome—even if using ranges instead of exact figures. Favor problem-solution articles over thin generalities. For links, pursue partnerships with reputable local associations, thoughtful guest features, and genuinely useful resources others will cite. Avoid buying links; long-term risk outweighs short-term gains."}</p>
    <p>{"Measure authority in practical terms: growth in relevant referring domains, quality of linking pages, and a natural anchor mix. Track brand mentions without links and request attribution where sensible. If services are your core, use the SEO for service businesses framework to balance conversion pages and supporting content. Expand topical coverage through interlinked clusters, each pointing clearly back to related services. Remember: a single link from a respected, topic-relevant local site often beats dozens from low-quality general directories."}</p>
    <h2>{"Local and multilingual SEO for Egypt and the wider region"}</h2>
    <p>{"If you operate locally, optimize your Google Business Profile, keep NAP consistent across your site and directories, and refine categories, descriptions, and photos. Build distinct location pages with addresses, hours, and genuine reviews to compete in the Map Pack. On-site, surface local pricing or currencies and region-friendly contact options. For multilingual audiences, provide a clear Arabic/English toggle, implement hreflang (e.g., ar-EG, en-EG, ar-SA), and adapt content to local context rather than translating literally."}</p>
    <p>{"Frequent mistakes include mixing dialects while targeting different countries or using international phone numbers that discourage local calls. Mind legal pages and privacy notices that reflect regional expectations. If serving both Gulf and Egyptian audiences, test speed from multiple locations. Don’t overlook trustworthy local citations and fresh visuals that reflect each branch. These signals help algorithms confirm you’re a real entity serving a specific area—and help people trust you faster."}</p>
    <h2>{"Measuring business impact, not just rankings"}</h2>
    <p>{"Tie SEO to business outcomes: qualified calls, consultation forms, bookings, or RFQs. Configure event tracking on key buttons, keep UTM hygiene in campaigns, and distinguish macro from micro conversions. Use Search Console to spot queries with impressions but weak clicks and adjust titles or content accordingly. Monitor lead quality, not just volume: conversion rate from inquiry to deal, average contract value, and time-to-close are crucial lagging indicators."}</p>
    <p>{"Expect a delay between publishing and results, so blend leading and lagging metrics. Build a dashboard that links high-traffic pages to pipeline they initiate. If a page drives visits from informational terms without inquiries, add logical next-step offers like a template, checklist, or consultation. Review data every 4–6 weeks and decide whether to update, redirect, or double down. SEO is continuous improvement, not a one-off project."}</p>
    <p>For a related practical guide, see: <a href={"/blog/technical-seo-audit-en"}>{"Related guide: technical seo audit en"}</a>; then <a href={"/blog/seo-for-service-businesses-en"}>{"Related guide: seo for service businesses en"}</a></p>
  </>;
}

